==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|-}}, from {{der|en|it|pasquinata}}, from {{m|en|Pasquino}}, name given to a statue in Rome on which lampoons were posted.<ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/paskwɪˈneɪd/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[lampoon]], originally as published in public; a satire or libel on someone.
#* {{RQ:Fitzgerald Great Gatsby|chapter=IX|page=196|passage=I thought the whole tale would shortly be served up in racy '''pasquinade'''—but Catherine, who might have said anything, didn't say a word.}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To satirize (someone) by using a pasquinade.
#* {{RQ:Poe Rue Morgue|page=168|column=1|passage=Chantilly was a quondam cobler of the Rue St. Denis, who, becoming stage-mad, had attempted the rôle of Xerxes, in Crebillon's tragedy so called, and been notoriously '''pasquinaded''' for his pains.}}

